{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>Therefore the children of Israel do not eat the sinew of the thigh, etc.</b> We find among the Sages that profound matters whose apprehension is difficult are likened to sinews (גִּידִין), as Rashi explained in parashat Yitro on the verse ״וְתַגֵּיד לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Exodus 19:3). And apprehension (הַשָּׂגָה) is called in the language of eating (אֲכִילָה), as we find in the matter of the four who entered the Pardes (Ḥagigah 14b), that concerning the hidden inquiry that is concealed from the eye of every living being he brings the verse ״דְּבַשׁ מָצָאתָ אֱכֹל דַּיֶּךָּ״ (Proverbs 25:16). And He hinted through this commandment for all generations to keep Israel from inquiry into hidden matters, as they said (Ḥagigah 13a) ״אֵין לְךָ עֵסֶק בַּנִּסְתָּרוֹת״ — 'you have no business with hidden matters,' for there is reason to fear lest they destroy their intellect and come to heresy, for not many are wise enough to understand all the secrets in their proper order, because their intellect passes through a valley of turbidity, for the vanities of this world and its lusts confound a person's intellect. And He hinted through the prohibition of eating the sinew for all generations, to be a reminder between their eyes that HaShem kept from them the fruit of the tree of knowledge — that they not feed their intellect things as hard as sinews, for he struck the hollow of Jacob's thigh; and if to Jacob the perfect one this stumbling occurred for a moment when he deviated somewhat from the path of equanimity, what shall the mosses of the wall do, whose dealings are mostly in the vanities of this world and its lusts? Therefore they shall have no business with hidden matters, except for the unique chosen ones of the generation such as Rabbi Shimon ben Yoḥai and his like, who loathed and despised the vanities of this world like Jacob. For only for a moment did this happen to Jacob, because he was left alone, and without doubt, after the matter of the embrace of Samael occurred to him, he sensed his sin and turned away from that path; and a proof for this matter is that it says ״וַיָּבֹא יַעֲקֹב שָׁלֵם״, and the Sages interpreted (Shabbat 33b) — שָׁלֵם, whole in his Torah. And this explanation is a precious and valuable thing, and the one who understands will hear and add lesson."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The phrase 'אֲשֶׁר קָצוּ וּמָאֲסוּ' is rendered 'who loathed and despised,' reading קצו from קוץ/קצה ('to loathe'); the text could alternatively be from קצץ ('cut off'). The expression 'אֵזוֹבֵי קִיר' ('mosses of the wall') is a rabbinic idiom for common, unexceptional people."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:31:54.835024726Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}